    "body": "Receiver compatibility scans every union declaration for each fallback query.\nThe module environment already contains an index for those same declarations.\nThis change reuses that index in `Scope.receives` and `Scope.has_method`.\nThe raw recursive `type_assignable` API and its array-based membership helper remain unchanged.\n\nThe lookup retains the first declaration with the exact union name.\nIt compares exact raw variant names, without suffix or visibility filters.\nIt adds no index construction, graph cache, or proof shortcut.\nDirect receiver and generic-base matches retain their order.\n\nPublished head `1a4847e9ebee824b2e0b972c5908c20b0fdc9057` retains compiler source `3d51e1880abc5025731f23772f1a343fe9c7fcbf`.\nIts compiler comparison baseline is main `515ddee8d0c06db2b94b4f3a86f3bf1c59cd2909`.\nThe clean merge retains main's struct-pattern and dyn-bound diagnostic repairs.\nBoth fresh normal compiler builds and both separate counter builds pass with the qualified A19 producer.\nAll production comparisons retain certification and normal verifier defaults.\n\nCurrent validation:\n\n- Pinned compiler source and format checks pass.\n- All 15 selected suites pass, with 145 test and subtest pass events and zero skips.\n- The package adds one terminal pass event and completes in 400.188 seconds.\n- New receiver membership controls pass on x86-64, QEMU ARM64, and WASI.\n- Controls cover duplicate order, empty and missing names, qualified variants, and direct/generic receivers.\n- Imported method collisions, ambiguity, enum methods, union methods, and checked-value refusals pass.\n- The upstream 4,000-function cost guard, callable values, and session replacement controls pass.\n- Feature census, complexity, Go vet, and complete CI self-tests pass.\n- The updated Go checker reports 2,387 test and subtest passes, with zero skips.\n- The full normal interpreter passes all 1,896 Opus records with the expected native-program TAP output.\n\nThe first integrated test attempt exhausted disk space at temporary-directory creation.\nThe retry uses the same source, tools, tests, and budgets with a dedicated temporary directory in shared memory.\nThe original logs remain intact.\nThe full interpreter control establishes correctness, without a paired speed or normal-run leak claim.\n\nEarlier source `99fda7a6` against main `0e715dff` records a complete cold Opus instruction comparison.\nBaseline records 16,923,232,536 instructions and candidate records 16,150,097,649, a 4.57% reduction.\nNative executable bytes match, and the full native reference program passes.\nThe original baseline copy lacked execute permission and stopped before compilation.\nThe corrected controller retains the successful candidate run and collects only the missing baseline half.\nThese earlier results do not qualify the current integrated source.\n\nPerformance qualification remains incomplete.\nEight fixed balanced pairs per workload and proof-cache mode will retain every CPU, wall, and RSS sample.\nThe workloads are complete native Opus and checker compilation.\nSeparate allocation and instruction controls will retain all losses and raw outputs.\nNo timing gain, allocation reduction, or world rank is claimed.\n\nThe earlier exact-head CI reports a two-core kernel scheduler failure.\nAll workers exit zero and frames return, but the test does not observe each worker on both cores.\nI claimed only this failure on #15118, comment 6773460833.\nThe published test fixture now requires each process to visit both cores before either core resumes it repeatedly.\nThe compiler, standard library, timers, worker, assertions, and budgets remain unchanged.\n\nSix ordinary current-main repetitions pass locally, so no natural local reproduction is claimed.\nOne private slower-timer control fails the original migration and concurrency assertions.\nThe corrected fixture passes 12 standard scheduler runs, Go vet, and every original assertion under that private control.\nThis control does not establish a unique cause for the CI timing difference.\nThe `ci-full` label retains every failure in the next complete round.\n\nMain then added the intrinsic-value diagnostic repair in `b442b6b9`.\nLocal merge `67b216378af4117cde0de6dc92a373a7636cccd2` preserves it and remains unpublished pending fresh qualification.\nThe fixed sweep continues on immutable sources `515ddee8` and `3d51e188`.\nEvery planned sample will remain, including slower samples and all outliers.\nThe new source checks, builds, and tests wait until that complete sweep finishes.\nThese source comparisons remain separate.\n\nKeep this PR in draft until performance qualification and complete exact-head CI succeed.\nRaw evidence and exact source identities will follow on this same branch.\n\nPart of #11534.\n",
